{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++ code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++ code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++ code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string s;\n    std::cin >> s;\n    \n    // Replace first \"at\" not at position 0 with \"@\"\n    size_t atPos = s.find(\"at\");\n    if (atPos != 0 && atPos != std::string::npos) {\n        s.replace(atPos, 2, \"@\");\n    }\n    \n    // Check if string starts with \"dot\" before processing\n    bool startsWithDot = (s.find(\"dot\") == 0);\n    \n    // Replace all \"dot\" occurrences with \".\"\n    size_t pos = 0;\n    while ((pos = s.find(\"dot\", pos)) != std::string::npos) {\n        s.replace(pos, 3, \".\");\n        pos += 1; // Move past the inserted \".\"\n    }\n    \n    // Restore \"dot\" at beginning if needed\n    if (startsWithDot) {\n        s = \"dot\" + s;\n    }\n    \n    std::cout << s;\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string s;\n    std::cin >> s;\n    \n    // Find all occurrences of \"at\" and \"dot\"\n    std::vector<int> atPositions;\n    std::vector<int> dotPositions;\n    \n    // Find all \"at\" positions\n    size_t pos = 0;\n    while ((pos = s.find(\"at\", pos)) != std::string::npos) {\n        atPositions.push_back(pos);\n        pos += 2;\n    }\n    \n    // Find all \"dot\" positions\n    pos = 0;\n    while ((pos = s.find(\"dot\", pos)) != std::string::npos) {\n        dotPositions.push_back(pos);\n        pos += 3;\n    }\n    \n    std::string bestResult = \"\";\n    int minLength = INT_MAX;\n    \n    // Try each \"at\" as the @ symbol (must be exactly one)\n    for (int atIdx : atPositions) {\n        // Check if this \"at\" can be the @ symbol\n        // It cannot be at position 0 or at the end\n        if (atIdx == 0 || atIdx + 2 == (int)s.length()) continue;\n        \n        std::string candidate = s;\n        \n        // Replace the chosen \"at\" with \"@\"\n        candidate.replace(atIdx, 2, \"@\");\n        \n        // Now replace \"dot\" occurrences, but be careful about positions\n        // We need to replace dots that are not at the beginning or end of the email\n        // and not adjacent to @ in problematic ways\n        \n        // First, mark which dots we should replace\n        std::vector<bool> replaceDot(candidate.length(), false);\n        \n        // Find all \"dot\" in original string and map to new positions\n        // We'll process from right to left to maintain positions\n        for (int i = (int)dotPositions.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            int dotPos = dotPositions[i];\n            \n            // Skip if this \"dot\" is at the beginning or end of original string\n            if (dotPos == 0 || dotPos + 3 == (int)s.length()) continue;\n            \n            // Calculate new position after @ replacement\n            int newPos = dotPos;\n            if (dotPos > atIdx) {\n                newPos = dotPos - 1; // because we replaced 2 chars with 1\n            }\n            \n            // Check if this dot position is valid for replacement\n            // Should not be at beginning or end of candidate\n            if (newPos == 0 || newPos + 1 == (int)candidate.length()) continue;\n            \n            // Should not be adjacent to @ in a way that creates invalid patterns\n            // @. or .@ are problematic, but .@ is more problematic\n            if (newPos > 0 && candidate[newPos-1] == '@') continue;\n            if (newPos + 1 < (int)candidate.length() && candidate[newPos+1] == '@') continue;\n            \n            // Replace this dot\n            candidate.replace(newPos, 3, \".\");\n        }\n        \n        // Validate the candidate\n        bool valid = true;\n        \n        // Must contain exactly one @\n        int atCount = 0;\n        for (char c : candidate) {\n            if (c == '@') atCount++;\n        }\n        if (atCount != 1) valid = false;\n        \n        // Must not start or end with @ or .\n        if (candidate.empty() || candidate[0] == '@' || candidate[0] == '.' || \n            candidate.back() == '@' || candidate.back() == '.') {\n            valid = false;\n        }\n        \n        // Must contain only valid characters\n        for (char c : candidate) {\n            if (c != '.' && c != '@' && (c < 'a' || c > 'z')) {\n                valid = false;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        if (valid) {\n            if (candidate.length() < minLength || \n                (candidate.length() == minLength && candidate < bestResult)) {\n                minLength = candidate.length();\n                bestResult = candidate;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // If no valid result found with the above approach, try to handle edge cases\n    // But according to problem, input should be transformable\n    \n    if (bestResult.empty()) {\n        // Fallback: try simple replacement of first valid \"at\" and all valid \"dot\"\n        std::string result = s;\n        \n        // Find first valid \"at\" (not at start or end)\n        size_t atPos = std::string::npos;\n        for (int pos : atPositions) {\n            if (pos != 0 && pos + 2 < s.length()) {\n                atPos = pos;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        if (atPos != std::string::npos) {\n            result.replace(atPos, 2, \"@\");\n            \n            // Replace all valid \"dot\"\n            for (int i = (int)dotPositions.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n                int dotPos = dotPositions[i];\n                if (dotPos == 0 || dotPos + 3 == (int)s.length()) continue;\n                \n                int newPos = dotPos;\n                if (dotPos > (int)atPos) {\n                    newPos = dotPos - 1;\n                }\n                \n                if (newPos > 0 && newPos + 1 < (int)result.length() && \n                    result[newPos-1] != '@' 